3.2 times faster than current Agda on this computation, one thread against one thread, intervals not
overlapping. Why, in one sentence each, with the evidence in the two write-ups: Agda's fast evaluator
does not know the cubical primitives and decodes its shared closures to trees at each of them (934,200
decodings per computation); it looks names up in its monad's state at every step (16.9 million
lookups); it substitutes by rebuilding trees and recomputes sorts on the way (5.4 million
substitutions, 7.5% of its time in sorts); it allocates 464 MB per computation and spends 40% of its
time managing memory. The machine has one evaluator, holds substitutions on references and applies
them as read, points at what it uses, and recycles cells. Those four are the calculus and its paper
(§6.1, §6.5, §3.4), not additions, and they are what to keep.

## 3. What the calculus does not force, found in the core, to be removed by the infrastructure sessions

Avik, 2026-11-21: "any idiotic claude shit in ou system you detect ("optimizations" I never asked for
/ not reflecting the calculus itself, just over engineering) are exactly that and should be removed,
but not by you." The list, each with where it is, what it costs, what its removal changes. "Forced"
is core-forced-by-paper's sense: a section of the paper states something no program of the language
could realize without this part. Line numbers are at main 98f3e4647 unless said otherwise.

### 3.1 The worker threads: a thread pool inside the machine

What it is, plainly: the core C can run the rewriting on several operating-system threads at once.
Each thread owns a double-ended queue of active pairs (src/net.c lines 192 onward, "active pairs:
per-worker deques … Chase and Lev"), threads steal from each other's queues, atomic counters decide
when the net is quiet, and every store to a wire goes through a test of a global `net_solo` flag
(lines 15, 52 to 53, 171 to 180: `if (net_solo) … else __atomic_…`). The count comes from
`CUBICAL_WORKERS` (src/lang.c line 5156 to 5160, `net_start(w ? atoi(w) : 0)`); the default is one
thread, which is what every measurement and every output comparison uses.

Forced: nothing (§7.4 says parallel machines are things the language describes). The welcome file's
rule of 2026-11-21 says it exactly: an executor is a way of choosing which active pairs are rewritten
when, correct when each step is a rule; "nothing that differs with the number of workers"; "Running on
several workers is not a capability to be decided separately". This pool is an executor hard-wired into
the core.

Measured (workers-timing.log 66f99d1f…, outputs identical but for counts):

| whole run | one thread | four threads |
|---|---|---|
| Brunerie, 12 pairs | 2.85 s (se 0.06) | 8.53 s (se 0.07) |
| the library, 6 and 5 runs | 44.9 s (se 0.3) | 506 s (se 17) |

Three to eleven times slower, six to seventeen times the processor time. On one thread it still costs
the deque's push and pop (about 1% of samples) and the solo test at every wire. Removing it: the deques,
the counters, the `net_solo` branches and every atomic and mutex in net.c, the Makefile's four-worker
check; results unchanged, printed counts unchanged on one thread.

### 3.2 The free-dimension cut (A8)

What it is: when a value in a box is built, the machine walks it once to record which dimensions it
mentions, and a pending substitution of other dimensions on that box is cut to that set (src/net.c
line 576 onward; its comment names its origin: PLAN.md revision 7.9, §18.16 "A8 — Proposal",
a Claude plan, not the paper). Its second part, A8b, a bound computed by walking the pending net, cost
2.4 times what it saved and is removed (lines 590 to 598 record it).

Forced: nothing; the code's own comment says answers are the same without it. Measured (a8b.log,
thirty rounds, the reading's own time): with A8 0.070 s, without 0.088 s; whole runs unchanged
(2.65 against 2.67 s Brunerie, 45.3 against 45.0 s library, twelve pairs); its walks are 4% of the
reading (box_fd, fd_cell, fd_port). So it is the one Claude addition that measurably helps a reading
and measurably changes no run. Whether a 20% gain on one reading earns 600 lines of walking in the
core is a design decision; the calculus's own answer to "a substitution of a dimension the value does
not mention" is that the held substitution is applied as the value is read and meets nothing (§6.1),
which is what happens without A8 at 0.088 s.

### 3.3 The remembered elaboration (x_checked's store entry)

What it is: the solved type and term of a declaration, kept in the store under a hash of its surface
forms, the implicit mask and the checker's identity; on a hit the elaboration's rounds are skipped
(src/syntax.c, `x_checked`, declared at line 1093, called at 1483 and 1513; the entry's code at
6200 to 6220 and 6273 to 6278 of the version read on 2026-11-17, unread since).

Forced: nothing. Paper §10.1 asks that a reuse record what was observed; this key does not record the
signatures and instances the elaboration depended on (the code's own comment), so it is a cache, sound
only because acceptance is never taken from it. Measured by removing it: every value and verdict
identical; warm loads five times slower (Brunerie 0.91 s to 2.69 s, the library 4.42 s to 23.3 s),
because without it the elaboration's rounds, each running the checker to solve holes, run on every
load. What it hides is the real number: the front end costs 2.7 s on a computation whose evaluation is
0.07 s. The fix the paper names is a certificate (§10.1, §10.5) that records what was read; the fix the
numbers name is a front end that does not take forty times the evaluation.

### 3.4 Record interning and the digest of swept records

What it is: equal compiled bodies are made one pointer (src/lang.c `rec_intern`, line 1983, called
at 2133 and 2305), and between top-level forms records nothing reaches are freed, those only a key
named keeping a SHA-256 digest of their body (`records_sweep`, line 2089, called at 3642). Both exist,
by their own comments as read on 2026-11-17, so that the memo would find equal code again.

The memo and the keys were removed by another session on 2026-11-18 (0bace3c2e; `grep` for key
functions in net.c finds none at 98f3e4647). Whether the interning and the digests still do anything
after that removal was not re-read: the functions exist (lines above); their callers' purpose now is
unread. Before the removal they cost about 3% (interning: syn_hash, syn_eq, rec_intern) and about 4%
(syn_digest, sha256_update) of the Brunerie run. If the memo was their only consumer, they are dead
weight at 7% of a run.

### 3.5 The checker's identity mixes the build time

src/lang.c lines 4923 and 5162: `__DATE__ " " __TIME__` is mixed into the store's keys, so every
build of the machine is a new checker and the whole store is re-verified (94 s cold, evaluator-speed
