## 4. Where the time goes now, for the next factor of ten

Avik, 2026-11-21: "our shit can prob go 10x faster than today, cause im better at algos than claude".
What the profiles say the time is spent on, so the algorithmic decisions are made on them.

The reading of the number (0.074 s; perf, realtime window, 1,120 samples, two-evaluators §5.2):

| share | part |
|---|---|
| 47% | the interactions: taking a pair, making and freeing cells, wiring, copying a value's top |
| 20% | the C allocator (malloc, free, consolidate) |
| 13% | substitutions and faces (compose, release, DNF meet and move) |
| 5% | building bodies from records |
| 4% | A8's walks |
| 11% | kernel and the rest |

The whole Brunerie run (3.5 s at e14aaba1, before the memo's removal; 7,234 samples; evaluator-speed):
interactions 17%, the front end (compiling, interning, strcmp scans) 23%, building bodies with their
keys 13%, interning and digesting records 8%, the allocator 12%. The library (54 s): interactions
30%, keys and memo 14%, front end 12%, allocator 11%, building 7%. These are before 0bace3c2e; no
profile of the whole run has been taken since the keys went. Taking one is the first measurement to
make.

The steps themselves, over the Brunerie run (8.7 million): erasures 3.15 M, case steps 1.69 M,
duplications 1.06 M, calls 0.79 M, views 0.67 M, substitutions 0.26 M, applications 0.25 M. Erasure
and duplication are nearly half of all steps: the graph's sharing is paid in copying and discarding
levels, at about 87 ns a step all included. The levers the numbers point at, as questions for the
algorithm's author and not as proposals:

- Erasure is 36% of steps. A value nobody reads is discarded level by level; whether erasure can be
  a constant-time drop of a whole sub-net (reference counts on roots, or an erasure that does not
  descend) is the single largest count.
- The allocator is 20% of a reading and 12% of a run on top of the slabs that already exist: the
  cells are pooled, but keys, entries, boxes, DNF nodes and substitutions were malloc'd (evaluator-speed
  item (4): "malloc'd keys, entries and boxes interleaved with the 64 KB cell slabs").
- The front end is the largest part the paper does not force: the Brunerie run compiles the checker's
  readings of all 260 definitions every time (readback inclusive 43% of the run), and the compiler
  scans four primitive tables by strcmp (120 million comparisons on the library). The remembered
  elaboration hides 2.7 s of this per load (§3.3).
- Substitutions and faces at 13% are the calculus's own work (§3.4, §6.1); their composition and
  release are where that 13% is, and whether a substitution held on a reference should be composed
  eagerly or kept as a list is an algorithmic choice this session did not measure.

## 5. Rules of measurement this session learned, already in the welcome file

A number is never one measurement: repeated, interleaved runs, with mean, sd and se; 100 rounds where
cheap. A comparison is against the current release of the other system. A mechanism is confirmed to
exist in the current source before it is switched and measured (the memo series of 2026-11-20 measured a
switch that another session had removed two days earlier). The reading's own time is measured by its
step counter under strace, not inferred from step counts. Agda's evaluator is profiled by a difference
(compute twenty times, subtract the load) or by a GHC-profiled build, which takes a day.

